{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 6, :comments ["<b>When he blessed him and charged him, saying: You shall not take a wife from the daughters of Canaan.</b> The deeds of the fathers are done by the children, and therefore he charged him not to take from the daughters of Canaan but only from his family and from his father's house, just as his father Abraham had charged him." "<b>And what</b> is written that he again blessed him — that is, what it says, 'and he blessed him and charged him' — the blessing is repeated several times, as it says, 'for Isaac blessed Jacob,' and again it says, 'when he blessed him,' in order to confirm and seal the matter, so that you should not say that he was blessed at first without knowledge; therefore he again blessed him here with his full consent. And this is what is stated in Bereshit Rabbah: because the blessings had been called into question, when were they confirmed in his hand? Here — 'and he blessed him.' The validity of a bill of divorce is only in its seal, so that you should not say that had Jacob not deceived his father he would not have received the blessings; therefore Scripture states, 'And Isaac called Jacob and blessed him' — end of quote." "<b>And behold,</b> here he sealed the blessing with which he had blessed him at first. And we find that the blessing flows from generation to generation since the creation of the world. Concerning the first man it is written (Genesis 1:22), ״ויברך אותם אלהים״. The world was cursed in the generation of the Flood and required a blessing, and He blessed Noah, as it says (ibid. 9), ״ויברך אלהים את נח ואת בניו״. They sinned in the generation of the Dispersion, and Abraham came and the blessings were placed in his hand. He said to him, ״והיה ברכה״ — 'you shall be a blessing' — you are the source of blessing; you may bless whomever you wish. Now Abraham should have blessed Ishmael, for he was the firstborn, and the custom among the early ones was that the blessing was in the hand of the firstborn, and the father would bless him. But Abraham did not bless Ishmael because the main substance of his seed was Isaac, as it says (Genesis 21:12–13), ״כי ביצחק יקרא לך זרע״. Nor did he bless Isaac, so as not to provoke jealousy between them. And once he withheld blessing from both of them, the Holy One, blessed be He, came and blessed him, as it says (ibid. 25), ״ויהי אחרי מות אברהם ויברך אלהים את יצחק בנו״. And Isaac should have blessed Esau, for he was the firstborn. And Jacob did that deed in order to take the blessings, and Esau lost both the birthright and the blessing, and it was emptied of both of them. When Jacob established twelve tribes, the blessings should have gone to Reuben, for he was the firstborn, but when he defiled his father's couch his birthright was given to Joseph. And since the blessings were fulfilled in Jacob, who is the third of the fathers, we have in this a great promise for our third exile, that they will be fulfilled in us in the time of redemption." "<b>And just as</b> we find that the prince of Esau consented to the blessings, as it says (Genesis 32:30), ״ויברך אותו שם״, so too in the time of redemption the kings of the nations are destined to consent to our blessings. This is what the Prophet promised us (Isaiah 49:23): ״והיו מלכים אמניך ושרותיהן מיניקותיך אפים ארץ ישתחוו לך ועפר רגליך ילחכו וידעת כי אני ה' אשר לא יבושו קוי״."], :book :genesis, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 28, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:15:52.827407736Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}